course: dictionary
lesson: 6742
title: Little word, many jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On is one of the most common words in English, and it can do many jobs. Most of the time it tells you where something is: «the tick on your trainers» means the tick is touching the top of your trainers.
explain: On can also mean a day, a switched-on light, or going ahead. The same word can change its meaning, so you need to read the words around it. Is something touching? Is it a day? Is something working? Those clues tell you which job on is doing.
explain: Be careful with the sentence «Get on with it!» It is rough talk that means hurry up. Hurry up is not about touching anything, so the words around on are your clue again.
fact: On means touching the top or side of something, as in «the chair you sit on».
fact: On shows a day or date, as in «on Monday».
fact: On means working or switched on: «the light is on» means the light is working.
fact: On can mean going ahead or carrying on, as in «read on».
fact: Informal: «You're on!» accepts a dare, «on me» means I'll pay, and «not on» (UK, Australia) means not acceptable.
exercise: «Almost everything you touch was designed by someone: the tick on your trainers.» What does on mean here? | It means working | It shows a day | It means going ahead | *It means touching the top or side of something
exercise: «The light is on.» What does on mean here? | Touching the top of something | *Switched on and working | Carrying on, going ahead | On Monday
exercise: «Read on» means what? | Shut the book | Read it out loud to a friend | Stop reading | *Keep going
test: «The advice of RoSPA is never to go onto ice on ponds, lakes, rivers or canals.» In «on ponds», on means what? | *Touching the top of, on the surface of | It means working | It shows a day | It means going ahead
test: «A book on sharks» uses on to mean what? | Switched on | Touching the top of a shark | On Monday | *About the subject of
exam: Which formal word can replace «on» in «on each occasion»? | Over | Onto | Under | *Upon
